  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1153 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nLow and behold, there are no big revelations with this afternoon's\nforecast package. As expected, we're running a few degrees warmer\nthan yesterday and thus on track for a hot afternoon as highs\nclimb into the mid 90's for the lower elevations. Once more,\naircraft-derived profiles show pronounced dry air in place,\nratified by the tongue of dry air aloft evident in water vapor\nimagery. We should have a touch more mid-level moisture later\ntoday relative to yesterday, enough for a few isolated high-based\nshowers mostly initiating off the higher terrain of the southern\nFront Range mountains and Palmer Divide, but with a lack of \nforcing these will generally fall apart as they transition into \nthe plains. In any case, some localized gusty outflows as high as  \n30-40 mph will be possible in their vicinity.\n\nFor Saturday, we eek out yet another slight increase in moisture \nunder southerly flow aloft, resulting in a modest rise in shower\ncoverage (still largely scattered for the mountains and urban\ncorridor). Enhanced easterly flow at the surface will support \nsome moisture advection into the eastern plains, pushing MLCAPE \nvalues into the 750-1,250 J/Kg range by late afternoon, creating\npotential for a stronger thunderstorm or two mainly for our\nnortheast plains. Bulk shear will be capped at around 25-30 kts\nhowever, limiting the severe potential to more marginal\nthresholds.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, we do manage to advect more appreciable\nsubtropical moisture into the region, leading to greater coverage\nof showers and storms. Synoptic forcing will remain limited\nthough, buffering the intensity of such activity. We never quite\nlose the influence of the high pressure even with the increase in\nmoisture, so temperatures will remain warm with highs climbing\nabove 90F for a majority of the plains and urban corridor.\n\nAs we progress through the work week, the ridge looks to flatten\nsomewhat but its axis also shifts westward over or at least \ncloser to Colorado, thereby maintaining above-normal temperatures.\nAt this point, the vast majority of ensemble members (> 85%) \nsupport the continuation of warm temperatures. If the high \npressure can remain relatively flat, we should still maintain \nsufficient low and mid- level moisture as it wraps around the \nridge to allow for daily isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms across most of the region.",
